---
<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} ogImage="https://help.xinca.com/articles/commercial-hvac-energy-code-compliance-hero.png" ogType="article">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)}></script>
  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold text-[#333] mb-4" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">{pageTitle}</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-[#666] mb-4">{pageDescription}</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-[#888] mb-8"><em>Last updated: 2026-09-14 · Methodology: every figure is traced to the producing source — government agencies, standards bodies, industry associations, audited company filings and peer-reviewed research — and each number links to the original document. Where research houses or report vintages disagree, all attributed figures are shown side by side.</em></p>
    <div class="not-prose my-8 rounded-xl border-l-4 border-[#2ea3f2] bg-[#f0f7fd] p-6">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#333] mb-3">Key Takeaways</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-[#444]">
      <li>Cooling accounts for around 10% of global annual electricity consumption but 30% of peak electricity demand (<a href="https://www.iea.org/commentaries/cooling-a-hotter-world-el-nino-meets-strong-growth-in-global-electricity-demand" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEA</a>, 2025).</li>
      <li>Global electricity demand for space cooling has reached about 2,900 TWh in 2025 — up 50% since 2015 and more than the total electricity demand of the entire European Union (<a href="https://www.iea.org/commentaries/cooling-a-hotter-world-el-nino-meets-strong-growth-in-global-electricity-demand" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEA</a>).</li>
      <li>200 million air conditioners were shipped worldwide in 2024 (<a href="https://www.iea.org/commentaries/cooling-a-hotter-world-el-nino-meets-strong-growth-in-global-electricity-demand" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEA</a>); world AC demand is estimated at 139,815 thousand units (≈139.8 million) for 2025 (<a href="https://www.jraia.or.jp/english/statistics/file/2608sh2_ac_world_estimates_2025_e.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JRAIA</a>).</li>
      <li>88% of U.S. households use air conditioning, and two-thirds use central AC or a central heat pump (<a href="https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=52558" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EIA</a>, RECS 2020).</li>
      <li>U.S. combined shipments of central air conditioners and air-source heat pumps fell 20.0% to 7,749,529 units in 2025 (<a href="https://www.ahrinet.org/system/files/2026-02/December2025StatisticalRelease.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AHRI</a>).</li>
      <li>European heat pump sales fell by an average of 21% to 2.2 million units in 2024 across 14 countries (<a href="https://ehpa.org/news-and-resources/press-releases/heat-pump-sales-drop-21-in-2024-leading-to-thousands-of-european-job-losses/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EHPA</a>).</li>
      <li>Heat pumps used as the main heating device cover around 10% of heating needs in buildings worldwide — over 100 million households (<a href="https://www.iea.org/commentaries/global-heat-pump-sales-continue-double-digit-growth" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEA</a>, 2022).</li>
      <li>The median annual wage for U.S. HVACR mechanics and installers was $61,010 in May 2025 (<a href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/heating-air-conditioning-and-refrigeration-mechanics-and-installers.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BLS</a>); employment is projected to grow 11% from 2025 to 2035.</li>
      <li>75% of employers globally reported difficulty finding skilled talent in 2024 (<a href="https://www.manpowergroup.ae/workforce-insights/2024-global-talent-shortage" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ManpowerGroup</a>).</li>
      <li>Air conditioning is Hong Kong's single largest electricity end-use: 49,077 TJ, or 30% of the city's electricity end-use (<a href="https://www.emsd.gov.hk/filemanager/en/content_762/HKEEUD2025.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EMSD</a>, 2023).</li>
      <li>Field measurements found 72% of air conditioners with improper refrigerant charge (<a href="https://www.aceee.org/files/proceedings/2004/data/papers/SS04_Panel1_Paper19.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ACEEE Summer Study</a>, U.S. field study).</li>
      <li>From 1 January 2025, new stationary residential and light-commercial ACs and heat pumps manufactured or imported in the U.S. must use refrigerants with a GWP of 700 or below (<a href="https://www.epa.gov/system/files/documents/2023-10/technology-transitions-final-rule-fact-sheet-2023.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. EPA</a>).</li>
      <li>Packaged rooftop units serve 46% of U.S. commercial buildings and over 60% of commercial floor space (<a href="https://www.pnnl.gov/main/publications/external/technical_reports/PNNL-23790.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PNNL</a>/U.S. DOE).</li>
    </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">What percentage of global electricity is used for air conditioning?</h2>
    <p>Cooling demand represents around 10% of annual electricity consumption worldwide, according to the <a href="https://www.iea.org/commentaries/cooling-a-hotter-world-el-nino-meets-strong-growth-in-global-electricity-demand" target="_blank" rel="noopener">International Energy Agency</a> (2025). Using air conditioners and electric fans to stay cool accounts for nearly 20% of the total electricity used in buildings around the world (IEA, <a href="https://www.iea.org/reports/the-future-of-cooling" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>The Future of Cooling</em></a>, 2018 report, 2016 data).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">What share of peak electricity demand comes from cooling?</h2>
    <p>Cooling accounts for 30% of peak electricity demand worldwide, despite its roughly 10% share of annual consumption — a mismatch that strains power systems during heatwaves (<a href="https://www.iea.org/commentaries/cooling-a-hotter-world-el-nino-meets-strong-growth-in-global-electricity-demand" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEA</a>, 2025).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">How fast is electricity demand for cooling growing?</h2>
    <p>Global electricity demand for space cooling has grown by 50% since 2015, rising to around 2,900 TWh in 2025 — more than the total electricity demand of the entire European Union; the figure covers space cooling in buildings and excludes data centres (<a href="https://www.iea.org/commentaries/cooling-a-hotter-world-el-nino-meets-strong-growth-in-global-electricity-demand" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEA</a>, commentary "Cooling a hotter world", 10 July 2026). Since 2015, cooling has accounted for 14% of global electricity demand growth, reaching as much as 25% in the Middle East and North Africa region (IEA, 2015–2025).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">Will demand for space cooling triple by 2050?</h2>
    <p>Not on today's trajectory: the IEA expects space cooling energy consumption to more than double by 2050 with no action taken (<a href="https://www.iea.org/reports/space-cooling-2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEA Space Cooling tracking</a>, 2022–2050 projection), while its earlier <em>Future of Cooling</em> analysis projected demand would more than triple by 2050 without action on energy efficiency — consuming as much electricity as all of China and India today (<a href="https://www.iea.org/reports/the-future-of-cooling" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEA</a>, 2016–2050 projection).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">How many households worldwide have air conditioners?</h2>
    <p>36% of households worldwide had air conditioners in 2022, a share the IEA's Net Zero pathway sees reaching 60% by 2050 (<a href="https://www.iea.org/reports/space-cooling-2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEA</a>, Net Zero Roadmap milestone table).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">How efficient are the air conditioners people actually buy?</h2>
    <p>The average air conditioner sold today is less than half as efficient as what is typically available on shelves — and one third of best available technology (<a href="https://www.iea.org/reports/the-future-of-cooling" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEA</a>, <em>The Future of Cooling</em>, 2018 report, 2016 data).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">How many air conditioners are sold worldwide each year?</h2>
    <p>Global AC shipments reached 200 million units in 2024 amid exceptional heatwaves (<a href="https://www.iea.org/commentaries/cooling-a-hotter-world-el-nino-meets-strong-growth-in-global-electricity-demand" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEA</a>). For 2025, the Japan Refrigeration and Air Conditioning Industry Association estimates world AC demand at 139,815 thousand units (≈139.8 million) — 106% of the year before, i.e. +6% YoY (<a href="https://www.jraia.or.jp/english/statistics/file/2608sh2_ac_world_estimates_2025_e.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JRAIA</a>, August 2026 estimates; room and commercial ACs, excluding portable and chiller types). Hong Kong/Macau demand is estimated at 539 thousand units in 2025, up from 523 thousand in 2024 (JRAIA).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">How many air conditioners does China buy each year?</h2>
    <p>China's air conditioner demand is estimated at 57,075 thousand units in 2025 — 41% of the world's total demand and roughly 115% of its 2024 level of 49,726 thousand units (<a href="https://www.jraia.or.jp/english/statistics/file/2608sh2_ac_world_estimates_2025_e.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JRAIA</a>, August 2026 estimates).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">What is Daikin's annual net sales revenue?</h2>
    <p>Daikin Industries — the world's largest air-conditioning manufacturer — achieved record net sales of ¥4,752.3 billion in FY2024 (April 2024–March 2025), 108% of the prior year, with the Air Conditioning segment at ¥4,384.5 billion (<a href="https://www.daikin.com/-/media/Project/Daikin/daikin_com/investor/data/kessan/20250508/presentation_2-pdf.pdf?rev=-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daikin</a> earnings presentation, 8 May 2025).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">How many heat pumps were sold in Europe in 2024?</h2>
    <p>Sales of heat pumps fell by an average of 21% across 14 European countries in 2024 compared to 2023, to 2.2 million units from 2.8 million; those countries make up around 90% of the European market, and the installed stock stands at around 26 million units (<a href="https://ehpa.org/news-and-resources/press-releases/heat-pump-sales-drop-21-in-2024-leading-to-thousands-of-european-job-losses/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EHPA</a> press release, 19 February 2025, preliminary figures). The 2024 dip followed the record year 2022, when 3.00 million units were sold across Europe — up 38.9% year on year (<a href="https://www.ehpa.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/EHPA_market_report_2023_Executive-Summary.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EHPA</a> Market Report 2023). At end-2022, 19.79 million heat pumps were installed in the EU, providing heating and cooling to around 16% of Europe's residential and commercial buildings (EHPA Market Report 2023).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">What share of home heating is met by heat pumps globally?</h2>
    <p>Heat pumps, when used as the main heating device, cover around 10% of heating needs in buildings today — corresponding to over 100 million households (<a href="https://www.iea.org/commentaries/global-heat-pump-sales-continue-double-digit-growth" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEA</a>, 2022). Global heat pump sales grew by 11% in 2022, marking a second consecutive year of double-digit growth (IEA, analysis published 31 March 2023).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">What percentage of U.S. homes have air conditioning?</h2>
    <p>88% of U.S. households use air conditioning, and two-thirds use central AC or a central heat pump as their main AC equipment (<a href="https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=52558" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Energy Information Administration</a>, 2020 Residential Energy Consumption Survey, published 31 May 2022).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">How many central air conditioners and heat pumps are shipped in the U.S. per year?</h2>
    <p>Combined U.S. shipments of central air conditioners and air-source heat pumps decreased 20.0% in 2025 to 7,749,529 units, compared with 9,681,770 units in 2024 and 8,656,674 units in 2023 (<a href="https://www.ahrinet.org/system/files/2026-02/December2025StatisticalRelease.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AHRI</a> December 2025 Statistical Release; 2025 split: 4,104,933 air conditioners and 3,644,596 heat pumps).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">How many central air conditioners and heat pumps are shipped in the US each month?</h2>
    <p>U.S. shipments of central air conditioners and air-source heat pumps totalled 707,436 units in November 2024, up 55.2% from 455,860 units in November 2023 (<a href="https://www.ahrinet.org/sites/default/files/Stat%20Release%20Nov%2024/November%202024%20Statistical%20Release.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AHRI</a> November 2024 Statistical Release). Year-to-date through November 2024, combined shipments were 9,162,444 units, up 11.5% compared with 8,221,085 units over the same period of 2023 (AHRI).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">What are the SEER2 efficiency standards effective January 2023?</h2>
    <p>Central air conditioners and heat pumps manufactured on or after 1 January 2023 must meet the new nationwide U.S. energy-efficiency standard, which keeps a regional approach at higher efficiency levels using the new metrics SEER2, HSPF2 and EER2 (<a href="https://www1.eere.energy.gov/buildings/appliance_standards/pdfs/2023_CAC_Standards_FAQ_10-5-2022_Final.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Department of Energy</a>, Appliance Standards FAQ; compliance date set by direct final rule 82 FR 1786, 10 CFR 430.32(c)).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">When must new US air conditioners and heat pumps use low-GWP (A2L) refrigerants?</h2>
    <p>New stationary residential and light commercial air conditioners and heat pumps manufactured or imported in the U.S. face a refrigerant GWP limit of 700, with the manufacture/import compliance date set at 1 January 2025 (<a href="https://www.epa.gov/system/files/documents/2023-10/technology-transitions-final-rule-fact-sheet-2023.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. EPA</a>, AIM Act subsection (i) Technology Transitions final rule, October 2023; sales of non-compliant products are prohibited three years after each manufacture/import date). The AIM Act separately mandates an 85% phasedown of HFCs from historic baseline levels by 2036 (<a href="https://www.epa.gov/hfcs/frequent-questions-phasedown-hydrofluorocarbons" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. EPA</a>).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">How much lower is the GWP of R-454B compared with R-410A?</h2>
    <p>R-454B (sold as Opteon™ XL41) has a GWP of 466 — a 78% reduction versus R-410A (<a href="https://www.opteon.com/en/products/refrigerants/xl41" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chemours</a> product literature, 2024–2026; R-454B is classified as an A2L refrigerant).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">How many R-32 air conditioners have been sold worldwide?</h2>
    <p>Approximately 480 million units of R-32 equipment are estimated to have been sold globally as of March 2026, of which around 66 million were sold by Daikin itself (<a href="https://www.daikin.com/sustainability/information/refrigerant/hfc32" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daikin</a>). Daikin estimates that if all R-410A refrigerant presently in use were replaced by R-32, the total CO₂-equivalent impact of HFCs could be reduced by up to approximately 800 million tons-CO₂ (Daikin).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">How many HVACR mechanics and installers are employed in the United States?</h2>
    <p>Heating, air-conditioning and refrigeration (HVACR) mechanics and installers held about 440,900 jobs in 2025 (<a href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/heating-air-conditioning-and-refrigeration-mechanics-and-installers.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics</a>, Occupational Outlook Handbook, base year of the 2025–35 projections).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">What is the median annual wage for HVAC technicians in the US?</h2>
    <p>The median annual wage for HVACR mechanics and installers was $61,010 in May 2025; the lowest 10% earned less than $40,050 and the highest 10% more than $95,210 (<a href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/heating-air-conditioning-and-refrigeration-mechanics-and-installers.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics</a>, OOH with OEWS data).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">How fast will HVACR mechanic employment grow over the next decade?</h2>
    <p>Employment of HVACR mechanics and installers is projected to grow 11% from 2025 to 2035 — much faster than the 3% average for all occupations — a projected increase of about 48,200 jobs (<a href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/heating-air-conditioning-and-refrigeration-mechanics-and-installers.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics</a>, OOH projections).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">How many HVAC technician job openings are projected each year?</h2>
    <p>About 40,600 openings for HVACR mechanics and installers are projected each year, on average, over the decade; many openings are expected to result from workers transferring to different occupations or exiting the labour force (<a href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/heating-air-conditioning-and-refrigeration-mechanics-and-installers.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics</a>, OOH projections).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">What percentage of HVAC technicians work for plumbing, heating, and air-conditioning contractors?</h2>
    <p>68% of HVACR mechanics and installers work for plumbing, heating and air-conditioning contractors — the largest employer group — and 6% are self-employed (<a href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/heating-air-conditioning-and-refrigeration-mechanics-and-installers.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics</a>, OOH, 2025).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">What percentage of employers globally report difficulty finding skilled trade talent?</h2>
    <p>75% of global employers reported they are struggling to find talent with the skills they need in 2024, a share that has risen steadily from 36% in 2014 (<a href="https://www.manpowergroup.ae/workforce-insights/2024-global-talent-shortage" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ManpowerGroup</a> Talent Shortage research).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">How does marketing spend relate to HVAC contractor profitability?</h2>
    <p>HVACR contractors who allocate at least 12% of their revenue to marketing and advertising have a net profit of +9%, compared with +5% net profit for contractors below that threshold (<a href="https://www.acca.org/news/release/acca-and-farmington-consulting-group-release-2025-contractor-of-the-future-study-results" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ACCA</a> 2025 Contractor of the Future study with Farmington Consulting Group; more than 1,000 HVACR contractors surveyed, 2025).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">What percentage of air conditioners have improper refrigerant charge?</h2>
    <p>Field measurements found approximately 72% of 4,168 air conditioners had improper refrigerant charge — outside manufacturers' performance tolerances of ±5°F for superheat and ±3°F for subcooling — and approximately 44% of 3,198 units had improper airflow (<a href="https://www.aceee.org/files/proceedings/2004/data/papers/SS04_Panel1_Paper19.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mowris & Associates via ACEEE Summer Study</a>, 3-year U.S. field study published 2004). Correcting refrigerant charge and airflow yielded average energy savings of 12.6% (same study).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">What share of US commercial buildings use packaged rooftop AC units?</h2>
    <p>Packaged rooftop units (RTUs) are used in 46% (2.1 million) of all U.S. commercial buildings, serving over 60% (39 billion square feet) of commercial building floor space (<a href="https://www.pnnl.gov/main/publications/external/technical_reports/PNNL-23790.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pacific Northwest National Laboratory</a>/U.S. DOE, report PNNL-23790, September 2015, CBECS 2003 base).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">What does a 25% refrigerant undercharge cost a rooftop unit?</h2>
    <p>A 25% refrigerant undercharge leads to an average reduction of 20% in cooling capacity and 15% in energy efficiency — about a 16% SEER reduction and a cost penalty of roughly $60 per year per ton at $0.12/kWh (<a href="https://www.pnnl.gov/main/publications/external/technical_reports/PNNL-23790.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PNNL</a>, citing Kim 2014). Improper economizer operations cost between $100 and $12,000 per unit per year depending on the problem and its severity, and PNNL notes a significant fraction of RTU economizer controls do not work as intended (Katipamula et al. 2003, cited in PNNL-23790).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">How long do AC condensers and heat pumps last?</h2>
    <p>The estimated useful life of an electric AC condenser (pad or rooftop) and an electric heat pump condenser is 15 years; electric furnace/air handlers and gas furnace/air handlers are listed at 20 years (<a href="https://www.hud.gov/sites/documents/eul_for_cna_e_tool.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development</a>, CNA e-Tool Estimated Useful Life table).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">What percentage of Hong Kong's electricity is used for air conditioning?</h2>
    <p>Air conditioning consumed 49,077 TJ of electricity in Hong Kong in 2023 — 30% of the city's 163,897 TJ electricity end-use and its single largest electricity end-use (<a href="https://www.emsd.gov.hk/filemanager/en/content_762/HKEEUD2025.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EMSD</a>, Hong Kong Energy End-use Data 2025 edition, data year 2023). Air conditioning also accounts for 49,092 TJ, or 18%, of Hong Kong's 272,656 TJ total energy end-use (EMSD, same publication, Table 11). Within sectors, air conditioning represents 29% of residential-sector energy and 24% of commercial-sector energy (EMSD, sector infographics; residential 61,365 TJ and commercial 122,796 TJ in 2023).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#333] mt-10 mb-3" style="font-family:'Trebuchet MS',Trebuchet,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif">What percentage of Hong Kong's electricity consumption comes from buildings?</h2>
    <p>Buildings account for about 90% of the electricity consumption in Hong Kong (<a href="https://cnsd.gov.hk/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/Climate-Target-of-HK_en.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HKSAR Government</a>, Hong Kong's Climate Action Plan 2050, October 2021).</p>

